{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.QPochhammerPartialProduct","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.QPochhammerPartialProduct","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_succ","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean#L137-L145","doc":"Recursive step of the partial product: adding one filtration\nlevel multiplies by exactly one new pole factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct_zero","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean#L133-L135","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.partialProduct","kind":"def","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean#L125-L131","doc":"The q-Pochhammer partial product `Φ_n(x; q) = 1 / (x; q)_n`.\nDefined via the recurrence `Φ_{n+1} = (1 - x q^n)^{-1} · Φ_n`.\nThe recurrence is the inductive filtration step on the algebraic\nside of the QOU framework. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:def:q-pochhammer-partial-product","label":"def:q-pochhammer-partial-product","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.AlgebraicCuspComplete","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.AlgebraicCuspComplete","kind":"def","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean#L103-L113","doc":"Algebraic cusp-completeness predicate: a Hecke normal-form word\nat GB-depth `n` is algebraically cusp-complete at substrate `q`\nand shape `x` when the denominator polynomial `(x; q)_n` vanishes.\nThe geometric reading (parallel transport around the matched\nideal-tetrahedron cusp yields holonomy 1) is recorded as a\nseparate complex-valued `GeometricShape` structure in\n`shared-denominator-cusp-holonomy.lean`; the present predicate is\nthe purely algebraic shadow. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean#L80-L101","doc":"**Root characterisation (biconditional).** Over an integral domain the\ndenominator `(x; q)_n` vanishes exactly when one of its factors does:\n`denomPoly q x n = 0 ↔ ∃ k < n, 1 - x q^k = 0`. The reverse direction is\n`denomPoly_eq_zero_of_factor_eq_zero`; the forward direction is where\n`IsDomain` is used (a product of nonzero factors is nonzero). Over a\nring with zero divisors the forward direction fails, so the hypothesis\nis not removable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_eq_zero_of_factor_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_eq_zero_of_factor_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean#L61-L78","doc":"The factor index `k` is a root of `denomPoly q x n` precisely when\n`1 - x q^k = 0`, i.e. when `x = q^{-k}` (assuming `q^k ≠ 0`).\nThese roots are the algebraic addresses of the geometric cusps\nthat bound the active JSJ piece on the archimedean side. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_zero","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean#L59-L59","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly_succ","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean#L55-L57","doc":"One-step unfold of the denominator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.denomPoly","kind":"def","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-pochhammer-partial-product.lean#L46-L53","doc":"Denominator polynomial `(x; q)_n = ∏_{k=0..n-1} (1 - x q^k)` as a\npartial product over the base ring. The roots of this polynomial in `x`\nare exactly `q^{-k}` for `k = 0, .., n-1`, and they are the\naddresses of the algebraic poles of the categorical state sum. ","signatureChunk":0}]}